<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yungang Grottoes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yungang Grottoes are Buddhist cave temples near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Datong</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, carved mainly between about 460 and 494 under the Northern Wei, whose capital was then Pingcheng. Begun under the monk Tanyao after a persecution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> ended, they contain about 51,000 statues, including five colossal Buddhas thought to represent Northern Wei emperors. They are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yungang-grottoes.jpg" alt="Yungang Grottoes" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:61292-Yungang-Grottoes_(28498548881).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The grottoes were formerly known as the Wuzhoushan Grottoes. After the decline of the Western Jin the northern parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> came under the Tuoba-ruled Northern Wei, who made the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Pingcheng</a>, now Datong, their capital, and construction there increased. The Northern Wei adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> early as their state religion. It reached the area by the North Silk Road, the northernmost route, about 2,600 km long, which connected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> to the west by the Wushao Ling Pass to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Wuwei</a> and emerged at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> before linking to ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The site lies about 16 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Datong</a>, in the valley of the Shi Li river at the base of the Wuzhou Shan mountains. The caves were excavated in the south face of a sandstone cliff about 2,600 feet long and 30 to 60 feet high. There are 53 major caves, 51,000 niches housing as many Buddha statues, and around 1,100 minor caves. The first period of carving lasted until 465, and these caves are now numbered 16 to 20. From about 471 a second phase lasted until 494, when the twin caves 5/6, 7/8 and 9/10, and caves 11, 12 and probably 13, were made under the supervision and support of the imperial court.</p><p>Imperial patronage ended in 494 when the Wei court moved to the new capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>. The other caves were made under private patronage until 525, when uprisings in the area brought construction to a final halt.</p><p>Cave 6 is one of the richest, with a surface area of about 1,000 square metres. Its whole interior is carved and painted, and a stupa pillar in the centre rises from floor to ceiling. The upper walls carry standing Buddhas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhisattva">Bodhisattvas</a> and monks among celestial figures. The caves have been repainted as many as twelve times, so the original scheme is hard to determine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Yungang is one of the three principal ancient Buddhist sculptural sites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, with Longmen and Mogao. The exposed sandstone has weathered heavily since construction ended. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> some statues were renewed, and the ten temples of Yungang, built from 1049 to 1060 to protect the main caves, were destroyed by fire some sixty years later. The wooden buildings in front of caves 5 and 6 date from 1621, in the late Ming.</p><p>Red Guards damaged many carvings during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>, and bullet marks are still visible. Since the 1950s cracks in the sandstone have been sealed by grouting. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> made the grottoes a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2001, calling them a masterpiece of early Chinese Buddhist cave art that represents the fusion of Buddhist symbolic art from south and central Asia with Chinese cultural traditions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yungang Grottoes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…and became great patrons of Buddhist art, influenced through the trade routes by India and Central Asia: the <b>Yungang Grottoes</b> near Datong were cut in the mid-to-late 5th century and the Longmen Grottoes outside Luoyang towards the dynasty's end, holding more than 30,000 Buddhist images of the period,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tuoba founded the state of Dai and then the Northern Wei, which unified northern China in 439, patronised the Buddhist cave temples of <b>Yungang</b> and Longmen, and under Emperor Xiaowen moved its capital to Luoyang in 494 and adopted Chinese dress, language and surnames.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
